<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kalabari Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1400 CE – 1884 CE (approx.) · capital Elem Kalabari</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Ijaw trading state of the eastern Niger delta whose 'canoe houses' dealt in slaves and later palm oil with European ships.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Kalabari tradition tells of migrants from the central delta who settled on an island in the eastern delta and founded Elem Kalabari, known to Europeans as New <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/old-calabar">Calabar</a>. Fishing and salt making gave way to trade with the hinterland by the 15th century, and Portuguese ships arrived soon afterward.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amanyanabo">Amanyanabo</a>, or king, ruled with the heads of wari, the trading houses that owned war canoes and absorbed slaves as members who could rise to lead them. Under King Amakiri, in the 18th century, Kalabari became a major slave port. After abolition it turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palm-oil">palm oil</a>. The Ekine masquerade society governed much of public ritual.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Rivalry with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bonny-kingdom">Bonny Kingdom</a> and a civil war led to Elem Kalabari's abandonment in the early 1880s, the people regrouping at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buguma">Buguma</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abonnema">Abonnema</a> and Bakana. Kalabari signed a British protection treaty in 1884 and became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/oil-rivers-protectorate">Oil Rivers Protectorate</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amanyanabo">Amanyanabo</a> still reigns at Buguma.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kalabari-kingdom.jpg" alt="Kalabari Kingdom" width="320" height="284"><figcaption>Image: CommonRollebon and commons · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IjawHistory.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1400 CE – 1884 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Elem Kalabari</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Amakiri I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Kalabari traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Kalabari</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/nigeria">Nigeria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Elem Kalabari, New Calabar</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1642&amp;focus=kalabari-kingdom" aria-label="Kalabari Kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4873 2007 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kalabari Kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5193 2263L5175 2243"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5175 2243h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5193 2263h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5193 2263h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1642&amp;focus=kalabari-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

